review_comments:
> Perhaps I misunderstood something here, but my intention for switching to synchronous cancel was to prevent such a situation

@rlubos Yes, you're correct the specific example of `write` and the work handler invoking `telnet_send()` is avoided with the switch to synchronous cancellation.  There appear to be other data races with state in `sh_telnet` though, for example the one above where one thread can be mutating the `output_lock` or `line_out.len` values while a different core in the work handler is using those values.  That too may not be true, depending on what threads can be accessing the data fields; if it is true then the synchronous cancel should precede modifying the shared state.

My point was just that the changes in this commit are primarily related to the API change, my review was limited to those changes, and that it's not necessarily clear that all race conditions associated with the use of k_work have been  identified and fixed (though one has, thank you.)
